 { With an extra pawn and the right to move Black seems to be easily winning, but this is not quite so. } 1... { -KEY- } Ke6 $1 { Only thus, obstructing the enemy king while approaching the queenside. } ( { The obvious } 1... Ke4 $2 { failed to } 2. Ke7 Kd3 3. Kd6 $1 { and White takes the 'upper' pawn first } ) 
 ( { while } 1... Kf6 $2 { would lose a crucial tempo: } 2. Ke8 Ke6 3. Kd8 Kd6 4. Kc8 Kc6 5. Kb8 b5 6. Ka7 $1 { and the white king approaches the pawns along the a-file. } ) 
2. Ke8 ( { Or } 2. Kg7 b5 $1 { prioritizing queenside action while the enemy king remains displaced; e.g. } 3. Kg6 b3 $1 4. axb3 b4 { followed by ...Ke5-d4-c3. } ) 
b5 $1 { In the same spirit as the previous note, progressing on the queenside while the white king remains obstructed. } 3. Kd8 Kd6 4. Kc8 Kc6 5. Kb8 Kb6 6. Kc8 b3 $1 ( { By swapping the pawns in this way, Black assures that when he captures the white pawn, his own pawn remains protected. We saw a similar trick ( } 7... h4 $1 { ) in the previous example. } ) 
7. axb3 b4 ( { In view of } 7... b4 8. Kd7 Kc5 9. Ke6 Kd4 { White resigned. } ) 
 ( { Finally, please notice that instead } 7... Kc5 $2 { lets White approach the king by } 8. Kb7 $1 { After } b4 9. Ka6 { Black could even lose the game, embarking on } Kd4 $4 10. Kb5 Kc3 11. Ka4 { etc, as observed by panuredu. } ) 

 0-1

