 { The following example shows that even top grandmasters sometimes fail to apply this idea. The awkwardly placed black king makes White's chances evidently better, but is it enough to win? } 1. { -KEY- } Kd4 $1 { Obstruction. } ( { Other moves lead to a draw; e.g. } 1. Kd5 $2 Kc3 2. f4 Kd3 ) 
 ( { or } 1. f4 $2 Kc5 2. Ke5 Kc6 { when neither } 3. f5 ( { nor } 3. Kf6 Kd5 $1 ) 
Kd7 4. Kf6 Ke8 5. Kg7 Ke7 { works for White. } ) 
 ( { In contrast, the game went } 1. Ke5 $2 Kc5 $1 2. f3 ( 2. f4 { returns to f4; } ) 
Kc6 3. f4 Kd7 4. Kf6 Ke8 5. Kg7 f5 $1 { (a timely sacrifice ensuring later opposition - we already saw this trick in Example 23, Brynell-Sokolov) } 6. Kf6 { and a draw was agreed in view of } Kf8 7. Kxf5 Kf7 ) 
Kb5 ( { Or } 1... Kb3 2. f4 $1 Kc2 3. f5 Kd2 4. f6 { (an idea familiar from the previous example: while the opponent's king remains obstructed, the stronger side keeps improving his pawns) } Ke2 5. Ke5 Kf3 6. Kd6 Kf4 7. Ke7 { and wins - Deviatkin. } ) 
2. Kd5 $1 { White repeats the obstruction in order to get his king to d6, where the enemy pawn will be at a shorter distance. } Kb6 ( { After } 2... Kb4 { White profits from his improved king: } 3. f4 Kc3 4. f5 Kd3 5. f6 $1 { followed by Kd6-e7. } ) 
3. Kd6 $1 Kb5 4. f4 ( { Adjusting the move order with } 4. Ke7 Kc4 5. f4 $1 { works as well. } ) 
Kc4 5. f5 Kd4 6. f6 Ke4 7. Ke7 { White wins. } 
