 { Now let's see two examples where the stronger side transposes to a pawn ending in order to prevail by obstruction. Despite the extra piece Black should proceed with care, since his king is remotely placed from its desired duty of protecting the h4-pawn. } 1... { -KEY- } Nd5 $1 { The pawn should be protected from behind, in fact this is a sensible idea in many knight vs. pawn endings. } ( { Less constructive is } 1... Kd3 $6 2. Kf3 Kd4 3. Kf4 { and White defends by opposition, or obstruction, name it as you like. } ) 
 ( { But } 1... Nc4 2. Kf3 Ne5 3. Kf4 Ng6 { is another possible move order, as in the later game T.Macsik-M.Zlatic, Paks 2004. } ) 
 ( { Even } 1... Nf5 $5 { intending the same plan of ...Ne7-g6 works, i.e. } 2. Kf3 Ne7 { transposing to the main line. } ) 
2. Kf3 Ne7 $1 { This results in an indirect sacrifice of the knight in order to divert the enemy king from the h-pawn. } 3. Kg4 Ng6 4. Kg5 Ke3 5. Kxg6 Kf4 $1 { This is it; with his king fatally obstructed from the pawns, White resigned. } ( { In contrast, } 5... Kf3 $2 6. Kf5 Kg3 7. Ke4 { would just draw - Shereshevsky. } ) 

 *

