 { With a pawn race coming up, Black should carefully study where to put her king on the next move. } 1... { -KEY- } Kc4 $1 ( { The only correct square for the king - we will soon see why. By the way, } 1... b4 2. Kxf7 Kc4 $1 { is another possible move order. } ) 
2. Kxf7 b4 3. f5 b3 4. f6 b2 5. Kg7 b1=Q 6. f7 Qg1+ 7. Kh7 Qf2 8. Kg7 { With the black king more remotely placed this would be a draw due to White having a bishop's pawn on the seventh rank, but here Black has an elegant path to victory. } Kd5 $1 9. f8=Q Qxf8+ 10. Kxf8 Ke6 $1 { Obstruction! This is why the king had to move exactly to c4 on the first move - Deviatkin. } ( { In contrast, } 10... Ke5 $2 { just draws after } 11. Ke7 Kf5 12. Kd6 Kg4 13. Ke5 Kxh4 14. Kf4 ) 
11. Ke8 ( { At the same moment, White resigned in view of } 11. Ke8 Kf5 12. Ke7 Kg4 13. Ke6 Kxh4 14. Kf5 Kg3 ) 

