 { It is not difficult to see that Black is in big danger here, but by accurate play with the king he managed to save himself. } 1... { -KEY- } Kg4 $1 { At first sight it seems wrong to block one's own pawn, but Mikenas has concluded that he must turn around his king in order to obstruct its white counterpart once the rook vs. pawn ending is reached. } ( { In contrast, } 1... Kh4 $2 2. Rc8 $1 Rh7 3. Ke6 $1 Rxd7 4. Kxd7 g4 5. Ke6 g3 6. Kf5 $1 { (king first!) } g2 7. Kf4 $1 { wins for White. } ) 
2. Kf6 { The trickiest try. } ( { After the analogous } 2. Rc8 Rh7 3. Ke6 Rxd7 4. Kxd7 { we can easily appreciate the point behind 1...Kg4: Black now has the obstructive } Kf4 $1 ( { or } 4. Kf6 Kf3 $1 ) 
 { with a draw after } 5. Ke6 g4 6. Rc4 Kf3 7. Kf5 g3 8. Rc3 Kf2 9. Kf4 g2 10. Rc2 Kf1 11. Kf3 g1=N $1 12. Ke3 Nh3 { etc. } ) 
Rf8+ $1 { The enemy king must be expelled from the g-pawn. } ( { Instead, } 2... Rh6 $2 { would let White queen after } 3. Ke5 Rh8 4. Rc8 ) 
3. Ke6 ( { Now } 3. Kg6 Rg8 { is useless for White. } ) 
Rd8 4. Rd5 ( { We already know that } 4. Rc8 Rxd7 5. Kxd7 Kf4 $1 ( { or } 5. Rd5 Kf3 $1 { draws. } ) 
 ) 
Kf4 $1 5. Rf5+ ( { Or } 5. Ke7 Rxd7 6. Kxd7 g4 { with similar play as before. } ) 
Kg4 6. Rf7 Kh3 $1 ( { Here, blocking the pawn by } 6... Kg3 $2 { loses a crucial tempo after } 7. Ke7 Rxd7 8. Kxd7 g4 9. Ke6 Kh2 10. Kf5 g3 11. Kg4 g2 12. Rh7 { and White wins. } ) 
7. Kf5 ( { Or } 7. Ke7 Rxd7 8. Kxd7 g4 9. Ke6 g3 { with a draw soon. } ) 
g4 8. Kf4 g3 9. Kf3 Kh4 $1 ( { Black avoids both pitfalls: } 9... Rh8 $2 10. d8=Q $1 ) 
 ( { and } 9... Kh2 $2 10. Rh7 Kg1 11. Kxg3 ) 
10. Rg7 Kh5 11. Kxg3 Kh6 12. Re7 Kg6 13. Kf4 Kf6 14. Rh7 Ke6 
 1/2-1/2

